{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update19.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (validate --run <run> --submission update19.json → valid=true). Closed the last external falsification route for the snapshot-install cycle finding, as a coverage negative. The fuzzy harness — the retained randomized suite that does stress partitions, membership changes and restarts — builds each node from `raft.DefaultConfig()` and then overrides the snapshot threshold to 1409600 entries and the interval to one hour (fuzzy/node.go:42-53), using a single Bolt store for both log and stable state plus a five-retention file snapshot store (fuzzy/node.go:62-68). Since no scenario generates anything near that volume and compaction only follows a taken snapshot, the leader's log stays complete for the whole run, a node returning from a partition is always repaired by log transfer, and the snapshot-send and install-snapshot paths are never entered. Recorded as one Fact plus an 'explained' interpretation with the coverage framing kept honest: this is a statement about what the retained randomized evidence constrains, not about the cycle's reachability or harm — it cuts both ways, since the suite's passing cannot be read as reassurance about snapshot handling nor as a refutation of the recorded account. Counterevidence preserved: restarts and partitions are still covered (just not snapshot creation/sending/installing), and the absence is purely due to two configuration fields, so changing them would change what the suite constrains. Together with the earlier records this completes the picture for that finding: the unit tests that do take snapshots cover only the index-behind premise, no scenario combines an explicit transfer with a non-voter, and the randomized suite effectively disables snapshots altogether.\"}"}}
